Titre$ P S I L O C Y B E    -    NOORDELOOS (2011)
Com$ STROPHARIACEAE -  Adapté par Paul Bertéa 31-10-2013

1,2 $ Fruit bodies usually distinctly bluing when handled and with age
1,12 $ Fruit bodies never bluing

2,0 $ Lamella edge easily separated from rest of lamella with a needle; on horse dung $ P. liniformans
2,3 $ Lamella edge not gelatinised; on dung or other substrates

3,4 $ On dung
3,6 $ On other substrates

4,0 $ Veil absent $ P. hispanica
4,5 $ Veil present in form of fibrillose patches at margin of pileus and a distinct annulus

5,0 $ Annulus thick, membranaceous, persistent; basidiocarps strongly bluing in all parts when bruised; spores 12.5-16 x 6.0-8.0 x 8.0-9.5 µm, ovoid to distinctly hexagonal in frontal view $ P. cubensis
5,0 $ Annulus thin, fibrillose, often disappearing with age; basidiocarps usually only faintly bluing, particularly in stipe; spores 12.5-15 (-15.5) x 7.0-8.0 x 7.5-8.5 µm, ovoid in frontal view $ P. fimetaria

6,0 $ Pileus dry with well-developed pileocystidia; pleurocystidia present $ P. puberula
6,7 $ Pileus slightly to distinctly viscid without pileocystidia; pleurocystidia absent or present

7,8 $ Grassland species, growing among grasses and mosses
7,10 $ Woodland species, growing on wood debris, woodchips, etc

8,0 $ Spores markedly flattened, subrhomboid $ P. gallaeciae
8,9 $ Spores not markedly flattened (width similar to breadth)

9,0 $ Pileus acutely conical or campanulate with acute papilla, hardly expanding with age $ P. semilanceata
9,0 $ Pileus expanding already in an early stage $ P. strictipes

10,0 $ Pleurocystidia scarce to abundant, lageniform or absent; cheilocystidia lageniform, with a shorter or longer neck (> 10 µm), fusiform or narrowly cylindrical, equal or flexuous $ P. serbica
10,11 $ Pleurocystidia present, frequent to abundant, clavate-mucronate, broadly fusiform or robustly lageniform; cheilocystidia lageniform, usually with short neck (< 10 µm)

11,0 $ Spores distinctly subrhomboid in profile view $ P. stuntzii
11,13 $ Spores ellipsoid to broadly elliposoid in frontal view

12,0 $ Spores ellipsoid to broadly ellipsoid, 10-12.5 (-13.5) x (6.5-) 7.0-7.5 (-8.0) µm, Q = 1.3-1.6-1.8; pileus usually up to 5 cm diam., bluntly umbonate with strongly undulating- wavy margin; stipe up to 10 cm long $ P. cyanescens
12,0 $ Spores ellipsoid, (11.0-) 12-13.5 (-15) x 7.0-8.0 µm, Q = 1.65-1.8-1.95, pileus up to 10 cm diam., acutely umbonate, not much expanding with age, never with strongly undulating marginal zone; stipe up to 20 cm long $ P. azurescens

13,0 $ Pileus dark greyish brown, when old also with olivaceous tinges; on needles and decayed wood of Picea in montane and boreal forest $ P. medullosa
13,14 $ Pileus differently coloured and in other habitat

14,0 $ Pileus vivid yellow with darker ochre-brown centre, in xerophytic grassland
14,0,N$/// ==> voir Leratiomyces laetissimus
14,0 $ Pileus dark red- or grey-brown; in peatbogs, often among Sphagnum $ P. atrobrunnea
